<!--
  Assignment 8: The Chain Rule, and Directional Derivatives and Gradient
  Vectors.

  The first four problems of the old worksheet "Exercises for The Chain
  Rule" (rev-tp-4, rev-tp-5, rm2-cr-1 and rw21-1), split out into an
  assignment at the instructor's request; its last problem (pp-29) went to
  review-problems-3.ptx.  Then the odd-numbered problems of the old
  worksheet "Exercises for Directional Derivatives and Gradient Vectors"
  (rev-gr-1, rev-gr-3, rm2-gr-2, rw23-3, rw21-8, rw17-8, pp-31,
  ex-electrostatic-plate and ex-isobars-low); its even-numbered problems
  went to review-problems-3.ptx as well.

  From that gradients worksheet: rw23-3 and rw17-8 were originally stated
  for functions of three variables and were rewritten for functions of two
  at the instructor's request (rw23-3 loses the vector (1,0,-1) and its
  gradient becomes (1,1); rw17-8 keeps its 3-4-5 directions with gradient
  (6,-3)).  In pp-31 the marked points sit at exact positions: P is the
  peak of the f=12 curve, Q is midway between f=2 and f=4, R midway
  between f=10 and f=12, and the f=2, f=0 and Q labels carry leaders.
  The paragraph introducing the three closing "two runners" problems was
  reworded, since one of the three (ex-fall-line) went to Review Problems.
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-assignment-8" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Assignment 8</title>

<introduction>
<p>
These problems apply the chain rule to functions of several variables:
differentiating a composition both by the chain rule and by substitution,
deciding a True/False statement about a rate along a curve, proving Euler's
relation for homogeneous functions, and working through a dependency tree
with two intermediate levels. They then use the gradient to compute
directional derivatives, find directions of fastest change, and read a
contour map. If you would like to review the material first, see
<xref ref="chapter-chain-rule" text="title"/> and
<xref ref="section-directional-derivatives" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="rev-tp-4">
<statement>
<p>
Compute the partial derivatives of <m>f</m> with respect to
<m>x, y</m> for
<md>
f(s,t) = s^2 - t^2, \qquad
s = \frac{x+y}{2}, \qquad
t = \frac{x-y}{2},
</md>
using both the chain rule and by plugging in <m>s</m> and <m>t</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Using the chain rule:
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}
\amp = \frac{\partial f}{\partial s}\frac{\partial s}{\partial x}
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial t}\frac{\partial t}{\partial x}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 2s\cdot\frac12 - 2t\cdot\frac12 = s - t = y</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}
\amp = \frac{\partial f}{\partial s}\frac{\partial s}{\partial y}
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial t}\frac{\partial t}{\partial y}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 2s\cdot\frac12 + 2t\cdot\frac12 = s + t = x</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Plugging in, we see
<md>
f(s(x,y), t(x,y))
= \frac14\left((x+y)^2 - (x-y)^2\right) = xy,
</md>
and easily obtain the same partial derivatives.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rev-tp-5">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="yes">
<p>
Consider <m>f(x,y) = \left(\cos x + (1+x)\tan y\right)e^{x^2-1+y}</m>.
Then <m>\dfrac{d}{dt}f(t^2, t^3) = 0</m> at <m>t = 0</m>.
</p>
<p>
Justify your answer fully: give a proof if the statement is true, or a
counterexample if it is false.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
Writing <m>x(t) = t^2</m> and <m>y(t) = t^3</m>, we
see that
<md>
\frac{dx}{dt} = 2t = 0
\quad\text{and}\quad
\frac{dy}{dt} = 3t^2 = 0
\quad\text{at } t = 0.
</md>
Now we see from the chain rule that
<md>
\frac{d}{dt}f(x(t), y(t))
= \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{dx}{dt}
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{dy}{dt}
= 0 \quad\text{at } t = 0.
</md>
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-cr-1">
<title>Homogeneous functions</title>
<statement>
<p>
A function <m>f</m> is called <term>homogeneous of degree
<m>n</m></term> if it satisfies the equation
<md xml:id="eq-rm2-homogeneous" number="yes">
f(tx, ty) = t^n f(x,y)
</md>
for all <m>t</m>, where <m>n</m> is a positive integer and <m>f</m>
has continuous second order derivatives.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Verify that <m>f(x,y) = x^2y + 2xy^2 + 5y^3</m> is homogeneous of
degree 3.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Show that if <m>f</m> is homogeneous of degree <m>n</m> then
<md>
x\,\frac{\partial f}{\partial x} + y\,\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}
= n f(x,y).
</md>
(Hint: use the Chain Rule to differentiate <m>f(tx,ty)</m> with
respect to <m>t</m>.)
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
If <m>f</m> is homogeneous of degree <m>n</m>, show that
<m>f_x(tx, ty) = t^{n-1} f_x(x,y)</m> for <m>t \gt 0</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<md>
<mrow>f(tx, ty) \amp = t^2x^2\,ty + 2tx\,t^2y^2 + 5t^3y^3</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = t^3\left(x^2y + 2xy^2 + 5y^3\right) = t^3 f(x,y).</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Using the chain rule we obtain
<md>
<mrow>\frac{d}{dt}f(tx,ty)
\amp = \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(tx,ty)\,\frac{d}{dt}tx
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}(tx,ty)\,\frac{d}{dt}ty</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-rm2-hom-chain" number="yes">\amp = x\,\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(tx,ty)
+ y\,\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}(tx,ty).</mrow>
</md>
On the other hand, the homogeneity condition
<xref ref="eq-rm2-homogeneous"/> shows that
<md xml:id="eq-rm2-hom-power" number="yes">
\frac{d}{dt}f(tx,ty) = \frac{d}{dt}\,t^n f(x,y)
= n t^{n-1} f(x,y).
</md>
Now we can equate the right hand sides of equations
<xref ref="eq-rm2-hom-chain"/> and <xref ref="eq-rm2-hom-power"/> and
set <m>t = 1</m> to obtain the desired equation.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
We take the partial <m>u</m>-derivative of both sides of the equation
<m>f(tu, tv) = t^n f(u,v)</m> (this is just
<xref ref="eq-rm2-homogeneous"/> plugging in <m>u</m> and <m>v</m>
instead of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>):
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial}{\partial u}\,t^n f(u,v)
\amp = t^n f_x(u,v)</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{\partial}{\partial u}\,f(tu, tv)
\amp = t\,f_x(tu, tv)</mrow>
</md>
Hence <m>t^{n-1} f_x(u,v) = f_x(tu, tv)</m> after canceling one
<m>t</m>, and plugging in <m>u = x</m>, <m>v = y</m> shows the desired
equation.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw21-1">
<statement>
<p>
Let
<md>
w = 5xy + 2yz - 3xz, \qquad
x = st, \qquad y = e^{st}, \qquad z = t^2.
</md>
Compute <m>\dfrac{\partial w}{\partial s}(-4,-4)</m> and
<m>\dfrac{\partial w}{\partial t}(-4,-4)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The dependency tree is shown in <xref ref="fig-rw21-tree"/>. We first
compute
<md>
\frac{\partial w}{\partial x} = 5y - 3z, \qquad
\frac{\partial w}{\partial y} = 5x + 2z, \qquad
\frac{\partial w}{\partial z} = 2y - 3x,
</md>
and at <m>(s,t) = (-4,-4)</m> we have
<md>
x(-4,-4) = 16, \qquad
y(-4,-4) = e^{16}, \qquad
z(-4,-4) = 16.
</md>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rw21-tree">
<caption>
The tree diagram for <m>w = w(x,y,z)</m> with
<m>x = x(s,t)</m>, <m>y = y(s,t)</m>, and <m>z = z(t)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="42%">
<description>
<p>
A tree diagram with w at the top. Three branches lead down from w to
x, y, and z. From x, two branches lead to s and t; from y, two
branches lead to s and t; from z, a single branch leads to t.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
  \node (w) at (0,2.4) {$w$};
  \node (x) at (-1.8,1.2) {$x$};
  \node (y) at (0,1.2) {$y$};
  \node (z) at (1.8,1.2) {$z$};
  \node (s1) at (-2.4,0) {$s$};
  \node (t1) at (-1.2,0) {$t$};
  \node (s2) at (-0.6,0) {$s$};
  \node (t2) at (0.6,0) {$t$};
  \node (t3) at (1.8,0) {$t$};
  \draw[mblue, thick] (w) -- (x);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (w) -- (y);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (w) -- (z);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (x) -- (s1);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (x) -- (t1);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (y) -- (s2);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (y) -- (t2);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (z) -- (t3);
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
By the chain rule, since
<m>\dfrac{\partial z}{\partial s} = 0</m>,
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial w}{\partial s}
\amp = \frac{\partial w}{\partial x}\frac{\partial x}{\partial s}
+ \frac{\partial w}{\partial y}\frac{\partial y}{\partial s}
+ \frac{\partial w}{\partial z}\frac{\partial z}{\partial s}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = (5y - 3z)(t) + (5x + 2z)\left(t e^{st}\right)
+ (2y - 3x)(0)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left(5e^{16} - 48\right)(-4)
+ (80 + 32)\left(-4e^{16}\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -20e^{16} + 192 - 448e^{16}
= -468e^{16} + 192.</mrow>
</md>
Similarly,
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial w}{\partial t}
\amp = \frac{\partial w}{\partial x}\frac{\partial x}{\partial t}
+ \frac{\partial w}{\partial y}\frac{\partial y}{\partial t}
+ \frac{\partial w}{\partial z}\frac{\partial z}{\partial t}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = (5y - 3z)(s) + (5x + 2z)\left(s e^{st}\right)
+ (2y - 3x)(2t)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left(5e^{16} - 48\right)(-4)
+ (112)\left(-4e^{16}\right)
+ \left(2e^{16} - 48\right)(-8)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -20e^{16} + 192 - 448e^{16} - 16e^{16} + 384
= -484e^{16} + 576.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<!-- ===== From the old worksheet "Exercises for Directional Derivatives
     and Gradient Vectors": its odd-numbered problems ===== -->
<exercise xml:id="rev-gr-1">
<statement>
<p>
Consider <m>f(x,y) = e^{-r^4}</m> where <m>r = \sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>.
Compute its directional derivative at <m>(0,0)</m> with respect to the
unit vectors in (polar) directions
<m>\theta = 0, \dfrac{\pi}{4}, \dfrac{\pi}{2}</m>. What about any
other angle?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The function is symmetric with respect to rotations around the origin,
so all the directional derivatives will be equal. Therefore it is
sufficient to compute the derivative for <m>\theta = 0</m>, i.e.
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m>:
<md>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}
= \frac{\partial}{\partial x} e^{-(x^2+y^2)^2}
= -2(x^2+y^2)\cdot 2x\, e^{-(x^2+y^2)^2}.
</md>
Plugging in <m>x = y = 0</m> we see that this is zero. Hence the
directional derivative at <m>(0,0)</m> is <m>0</m> in the directions
<m>\theta = 0, \dfrac{\pi}{4}, \dfrac{\pi}{2}</m>, and by the same
symmetry it is <m>0</m> for every other angle as well.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rev-gr-3">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="yes">
<p>
If <m>\hat{u} = \langle a, b\rangle</m> is a unit vector and <m>f</m>
has continuous second partials, then
<md>
D^2_{\hat{u}} f(x,y) = f_{xx}a^2 + 2f_{xy}ab + f_{yy}b^2,
</md>
where <m>D^2_{\hat{u}} f = D_{\hat{u}}\left[D_{\hat{u}} f(x,y)\right]</m>.
</p>
<p>
Justify your answer fully: give a proof if the statement is true, or a
counterexample if it is false.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
We have that
<md>
D^2_{\hat{u}} f
= D_{\hat{u}}\left[D_{\hat{u}} f(x,y)\right]
= D_{\hat{u}}\left[a f_x + b f_y\right].
</md>
This simplifies to
<md>
\langle a f_{xx} + b f_{xy},\; a f_{xy} + b f_{yy}\rangle
\cdot \langle a, b\rangle
= f_{xx}a^2 + 2f_{xy}ab + f_{yy}b^2.
</md>
Note we needed <m>f</m> to have continuous second partials to invoke
Clairaut's Theorem.
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-gr-2">
<title>A mountain lion on a mountain</title>
<statement>
<p>
A mountain lion runs on a mountain whose height above the point
<m>(x,y)</m> is <m>z = x^2 + \sin^2(xy)</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
In which direction(s) should the mountain lion run from point
<m>(1, 0, 1)</m> so that the height is increasing at the fastest
possible rate? What is this fastest rate?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
In which direction(s) should the mountain lion run from point
<m>(1, 0, 1)</m> so that the height is increasing at <em>half</em> of
the fastest possible rate?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The fastest possible rate of increase is attained in the direction of
the gradient:
<md>
\nabla z = \left\langle \frac{\partial z}{\partial x},
\frac{\partial z}{\partial y}\right\rangle
= \left\langle 2x + 2\sin(xy)\cos(xy)\,y,\;
2\sin(xy)\cos(xy)\,x\right\rangle.
</md>
At <m>(1, 0, 1)</m>, this gradient is simply
<m>\nabla z(1,0) = \langle 2, 0\rangle</m>, and the fastest possible
rate of change is the length of the gradient, i.e.
<m>\|\nabla z(1,0)\| = \|\langle 2,0\rangle\| = 2</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Half of the fastest possible rate of height increase at point
<m>(1,0,1)</m> is <m>\frac12 \cdot 2 = 1</m>. Let
<m>\hat{u} = \langle a, b\rangle</m> be a unit vector in the desired
direction. Then the directional derivative
<m>D_{\hat{u}}(1,0) = 1</m> is the desired rate of change of height at
<m>(1,0,1)</m> in the direction of <m>\hat{u}</m>; it can be calculated
using the dot product with the gradient:
<md>
1 = D_{\hat{u}}(1,0)
= \hat{u} \cdot \nabla z(1,0)
= \langle a, b\rangle \cdot \langle 2, 0\rangle
= 2a + 0b = 2a.
</md>
Thus, <m>a = \frac12</m> and <m>b</m> must be such that
<m>\hat{u} = \left\langle \frac12, b\right\rangle</m> is unit, i.e.
<m>\left(\frac12\right)^2 + b^2 = 1</m>, from which
<m>b^2 = \frac34</m> and <m>b = \pm\frac{\sqrt3}{2}</m>. Therefore,
the desired directions in which the height is increasing at a rate
equal to half of the fastest possible rate are
<m>\hat{u}_1 = \left\langle \frac12, \frac{\sqrt3}{2}\right\rangle</m>
and
<m>\hat{u}_2 = \left\langle \frac12, -\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\right\rangle</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw23-3">
<statement>
<p>
Suppose that <m>f(x,y)</m> is a function of two variables and let
<md>
\hat u = \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\langle 1, -1\rangle, \qquad
\hat v = \langle 0, 1\rangle.
</md>
Suppose that at a point <m>P_0(a,b)</m>,
<md>
<mrow>\left(D_{\hat u} f\right)_{P_0} \amp = 0,</mrow>
<mrow>\left(D_{\hat v} f\right)_{P_0} \amp = 1.</mrow>
</md>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find <em>the direction</em> of the maximum rate of change at
<m>P_0(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the maximum rate of change at <m>P_0(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Using
<m>\left(D_{\hat u} f\right)_{P_0}
= \nabla f\big|_{P_0}\cdot\hat u</m>, the two conditions become
<md>
<mrow>\left(D_{\hat u} f\right)_{P_0} = 0
\;\Rightarrow\;
\langle f_x, f_y\rangle \cdot
\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\langle 1, -1\rangle
= \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\left(f_x - f_y\right) = 0</mrow>
<mrow>\qquad \Rightarrow\; f_x = f_y,</mrow>
<mrow>\left(D_{\hat v} f\right)_{P_0} = 1
\;\Rightarrow\;
\langle f_x, f_y\rangle \cdot \langle 0, 1\rangle
= f_y = 1.</mrow>
</md>
Hence <m>\nabla f\big|_{P_0} = \langle 1, 1\rangle</m>, and the
direction of the maximum rate of change at <m>P_0</m> is given by
<md>
\hat u = \frac{\nabla f\big|_{P_0}}{\left\|\nabla f\big|_{P_0}\right\|}
= \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\langle 1, 1\rangle.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\left\|\nabla f\big|_{P_0}\right\| = \sqrt{1 + 1} = \sqrt2</m>
is the maximum rate of change.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw21-8">
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = x^2 e^{3y}</m> and the point
<m>P_0 = (-1, 0)</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find the direction in which <m>f(x,y)</m> decreases the most rapidly
at <m>P_0</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the directional derivative of <m>f(x,y)</m> at <m>P_0</m> in the
direction given by <m>\overrightarrow{v} = \langle -1, 1\rangle</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The gradient is
<md>
<mrow>\nabla f \amp = \left(2x e^{3y}\right)\hat{\imath}
+ \left(3x^2 e^{3y}\right)\hat{\jmath},</mrow>
<mrow>\nabla f\big|_{(-1,0)} \amp = -2\,\hat{\imath} + 3\,\hat{\jmath},
\qquad
\left\|\nabla f\big|_{(-1,0)}\right\| = \sqrt{13}.</mrow>
</md>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The direction of most rapid decrease is opposite to the gradient:
<md>
-\frac{\nabla f}{\|\nabla f\|}
= \left(\frac{2}{\sqrt{13}}\right)\hat{\imath}
- \left(\frac{3}{\sqrt{13}}\right)\hat{\jmath}
= \left\langle \frac{2}{\sqrt{13}}, -\frac{3}{\sqrt{13}}
\right\rangle.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The unit vector in the direction of <m>\overrightarrow{v}</m> is
<m>\hat u = \dfrac{\overrightarrow{v}}{\|\overrightarrow{v}\|}
= \left\langle -\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}, \dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}\right\rangle</m>,
so
<md>
D_{\hat u} f
= \nabla f \cdot \hat u
= \langle -2, 3\rangle \cdot
\left\langle -\frac{1}{\sqrt2}, \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\right\rangle
= \frac{5}{\sqrt2}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw17-8">
<statement>
<p>
Suppose the temperature (in degrees Celsius) at a point
<m>(x,y)</m> is given by <m>T(x,y)</m>, where <m>T</m> is a
continuous function that has continuous partial derivatives, and
distances in the <m>xy</m>-plane are measured in meters. We are given
the following information about <m>T</m> at the point
<m>(x,y) = (1,1)</m>:
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<m>T(1,1) = 20</m> <degree/>C,
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
the derivative of <m>T</m> at <m>(1,1)</m> obtains its smallest
(i.e. most negative) value in the direction toward the <em>point</em>
<m>(-1, 2)</m>,
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
the derivative of <m>T</m> at <m>(1,1)</m> in the direction of the
<em>vector</em> <m>4\,\hat{\imath} + 3\,\hat{\jmath}</m> is
<m>3</m> <degree/>C/m.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find the derivative of <m>T</m> at <m>(1,1)</m> in the direction
toward the <em>point</em> <m>(4,-3)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
If you start at <m>(1,1)</m> and move along a straight path toward
the point <m>(4,-3)</m>, <em>approximately</em> how far will you
have to go in this direction until the temperature reaches
<m>24</m> <degree/>C?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
First we determine <m>\nabla T</m> at <m>(1,1)</m>. The derivative
is most negative in the direction of <m>-\nabla T</m>, and the
direction from <m>(1,1)</m> toward <m>(-1,2)</m> is
<md>
\langle -1-1,\; 2-1\rangle = \langle -2, 1\rangle.
</md>
Hence <m>-\nabla T</m> is a positive multiple of
<m>\langle -2, 1\rangle</m>, i.e.
<m>\nabla T = c\,\langle 2, -1\rangle</m> for some <m>c \gt 0</m>.
</p>

<p>
The third piece of information fixes <m>c</m>. The unit vector in the
direction of <m>4\,\hat{\imath} + 3\,\hat{\jmath}</m> is
<m>\frac15\langle 4, 3\rangle</m>, since
<m>\|\langle 4,3\rangle\| = 5</m>, so
<md>
3 = \nabla T \cdot \frac15\langle 4, 3\rangle
= \frac{c}{5}\Big(8 - 3\Big)
= c
\;\Rightarrow\; c = 3.
</md>
Therefore
<md>
\nabla T\big|_{(1,1)}
= 3\langle 2, -1\rangle
= \langle 6, -3\rangle.
</md>
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The direction from <m>(1,1)</m> toward <m>(4,-3)</m> is
<m>\langle 3, -4\rangle</m>, whose length is <m>5</m>, so the unit
vector is <m>\hat u = \left\langle \frac35, -\frac45\right\rangle</m>
and
<md>
\left(D_{\hat u} T\right)_{(1,1)}
= \langle 6, -3\rangle \cdot
\left\langle \frac35, -\frac45\right\rangle
= \frac{18}{5} + \frac{12}{5}
= 6 \; {}^{\circ}\text{C}/\text{m}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The temperature must rise from <m>20</m> <degree/>C to <m>24</m>
<degree/>C, i.e. <m>\Delta T = 4</m> <degree/>C, and along this
direction it rises at approximately <m>6</m> <degree/>C per meter.
Using the linear approximation
<m>\Delta T \approx \left(D_{\hat u}T\right) \cdot s</m>, where
<m>s</m> is the distance travelled,
<md>
s \approx \frac{\Delta T}{D_{\hat u}T} = \frac46 = \frac23
\approx 0.67 \text{ meters}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-31">
<statement>
<p>
<m>f</m> is a <q>nice</q> function of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> (i.e.
<m>f</m> is continuous and its partials exist). In
<xref ref="fig-pp-contours"/> are shown the level curves
<m>f(x,y) = 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Let <m>u_1 = \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\hat{\imath} + \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\hat{\jmath}</m>.
Estimate <m>D_{u_1}f(4,3)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Estimate <m>D_{u_1}f(1,2)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Draw on the diagram a unit vector pointing in the same direction as
<m>\nabla f(1,2)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
At <m>P</m>, draw a unit vector and label it <m>u_2</m>, such that
<m>D_{u_2}f(P) \lt 0</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Estimate <m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x}(P)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Which appears bigger, <m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}(Q)</m> or
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}(R)</m>?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pp-contours">
<caption>
Level curves of <m>f</m> at the values
<m>0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12</m>. The marked point <m>P</m> is the highest
point of the <m>f = 12</m> curve; <m>Q</m> lies midway between the
<m>f = 2</m> and <m>f = 4</m> curves; <m>R</m> lies midway between the
<m>f = 10</m> and <m>f = 12</m> curves.
</caption>
<image width="88%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-pp-contours">
<diagram dimensions="(470,420)" margins="12">
<definition>c0(t) = (t, -0.30 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c2(t) = (t, -0.08 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c4(t) = (t, 0.17 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c6(t) = (t, 0.75 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c8(t) = (t, 1.88 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c10(t) = (t, 2.98 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c12(t) = (t, 3.90 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-1.35,8.3,6.6)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-0" function="c0" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-2" function="c2" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-4" function="c4" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-6" function="c6" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-8" function="c8" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-10" function="c10" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-12" function="c12" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<!-- P is the peak of the f=12 curve; Q is midway between f=2 and f=4,
     R midway between f=10 and f=12, so each point's position is exact. -->
<point at="ct-P" p="(2.4,5.697)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="ct-Q" p="(5.2,0.59)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="ct-R" p="(4.6,4.298)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<line at="ct-Q-lead" endpoints="((5.24,0.64),(5.42,0.86))"
      stroke="#D55E00" thickness="1.5"/>
<label at="ct-P-lab" anchor="(2.4,5.82)" alignment="n" color="#D55E00">
<m>P</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-Q-lab" anchor="(5.45,0.90)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>Q</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-R-lab" anchor="(4.72,4.30)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>R</m>
</label>
<!-- The f=2 and f=0 labels do not fit beside their curves, so each is
     set below and joined to its curve by a leader. -->
<line at="ct-lead2" endpoints="((6.35,0.114),(6.70,-0.41))"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1"/>
<line at="ct-lead0" endpoints="((6.35,-0.106),(6.70,-1.01))"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1"/>
<label at="ct-l12" anchor="(6.45,4.094)" alignment="e">
<m>f=12</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l10" anchor="(6.45,3.174)" alignment="e">
<m>f=10</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l8" anchor="(6.45,2.074)" alignment="e">
<m>f=8</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l6" anchor="(6.45,0.944)" alignment="e">
<m>f=6</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l4" anchor="(6.45,0.364)" alignment="e">
<m>f=4</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l2" anchor="(6.75,-0.45)" alignment="e">
<m>f=2</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l0" anchor="(6.75,-1.05)" alignment="e">
<m>f=0</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Seven level curves of f, labelled zero through twelve from the bottom up. Each curve rises steeply from the left, reaches a hump near x equals two point four, and then descends gently to the right. The low valued curves are bunched tightly together near the x axis on the right, where the point Q sits, while the high valued curves are widely spaced near the top right, where the point R sits. The point P sits at the very top of the f equals twelve curve, where that curve is horizontal.">
  <annotation ref="ct-P" text="The point P, on the f equals twelve level curve at its highest point, where that curve is horizontal."/>
  <annotation ref="ct-Q" text="The point Q, at the lower right, exactly midway between the f equals two and f equals four level curves, which are only about one quarter of a unit apart."/>
  <annotation ref="ct-R" text="The point R, at the upper right, exactly midway between the f equals ten and f equals twelve level curves, which are about one unit apart."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The directional derivative <m>D_{u_1}f(4,3)</m> tells us roughly how
much <m>f</m> increases if we move <em>one unit</em> from
<m>(4,3)</m> in the direction <m>u_1</m>, i.e. diagonally up and to
the right at <m>45^{\circ}</m>. Starting at <m>(4,3)</m> and moving
one unit that way lands us near <m>(4.7, 3.7)</m>, which is about two
contours further along, and each contour step is <m>\Delta f = 2</m>
spread over roughly one unit of travel. Reading the map,
<md>
D_{u_1}f(4,3) \approx 2.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
At <m>(1,2)</m> the level curve through that point is climbing at
roughly <m>45^{\circ}</m>, i.e. the level curve is
<em>parallel</em> to <m>u_1</m>. Moving along a level curve does not
change the value of <m>f</m>, so
<md>
D_{u_1}f(1,2) \approx 0.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The gradient is perpendicular to the level curve and points in the
direction of <em>increase</em>. At <m>(1,2)</m> the level curve runs
up and to the right at about <m>45^{\circ}</m>, and <m>f</m>
increases as we move upward, so <m>\nabla f(1,2)</m> points up and to
the <em>left</em>, i.e. in the direction
<m>\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\langle -1, 1\rangle</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-pp-contours-answer"/>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
We need any direction of <em>decrease</em> at <m>P</m>, i.e. any
direction pointing toward the lower-valued level curves (toward
<m>f = 10</m>, not away from it). Since the level curves below
<m>P</m> have smaller values, <m>u_2 = \langle 0, -1\rangle</m>
(straight down) works. See <xref ref="fig-pp-contours-answer"/>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>P</m> sits at the very top of its level curve, where the curve is
<em>horizontal</em>. Walking in the <m>+x</m> direction from
<m>P</m> therefore keeps us on the same level curve for a while, so
<m>f</m> does not change to first order:
<md>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(P) \approx 0.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m> measures how fast <m>f</m>
changes as we move straight up, which is large where the contours are
close together and small where they are far apart. At <m>Q</m> we only
have to go up about <m>0.25</m> units to move from one contour to the
next, so
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}(Q) \approx \dfrac{2}{0.25} = 8</m>.
At <m>R</m> we must go up about <m>1</m> unit for the same change, so
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}(R) \approx \dfrac{2}{1} = 2</m>.
Hence <m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}(Q)</m> is bigger.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pp-contours-answer">
<caption>
The vectors asked for in parts C and D: <m>\nabla f(1,2)</m> points
perpendicular to the level curve toward increasing <m>f</m>, while
<m>u_2</m> at <m>P</m> points toward decreasing <m>f</m>.
</caption>
<image width="88%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-pp-contours-answer">
<diagram dimensions="(470,420)" margins="12">
<definition>c0(t) = (t, -0.30 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c2(t) = (t, -0.08 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c4(t) = (t, 0.17 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c6(t) = (t, 0.75 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c8(t) = (t, 1.88 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c10(t) = (t, 2.98 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c12(t) = (t, 3.90 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-1.35,8.3,6.6)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="ca-0" function="c0" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="ca-2" function="c2" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="ca-4" function="c4" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="ca-6" function="c6" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="ca-8" function="c8" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="ca-10" function="c10" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="ca-12" function="c12" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.5"/>
<vector at="ca-grad" v="(-0.71,0.71)" tail="(1,2)"
        stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="ca-u2" v="(0,-1)" tail="(2.4,5.697)"
        stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<point at="ca-pt12" p="(1,2)" fill="#009E73"/>
<point at="ca-P" p="(2.4,5.697)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<label at="ca-grad-lab" anchor="(0.34,2.84)" alignment="ne" color="#009E73">
<m>\nabla f(1,2)</m>
</label>
<label at="ca-u2-lab" anchor="(2.62,4.82)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>u_2</m>
</label>
<label at="ca-P-lab" anchor="(2.4,5.82)" alignment="n" color="#D55E00">
<m>P</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The same seven level curves, drawn in gray. A green arrow at the point one comma two points up and to the left, perpendicular to the level curve there; it is labelled gradient of f at one comma two. A vermillion arrow at P points straight down and is labelled u two.">
  <annotation ref="ca-grad" text="The gradient at one comma two, perpendicular to the level curve and pointing toward larger values of f."/>
  <annotation ref="ca-u2" text="The vector u two at P, pointing straight down toward smaller values of f, so the directional derivative there is negative."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<p>
The two problems that close this assignment repeat the three questions of
<xref ref="ex-two-runners"/> in two more physical settings: find the
direction of most rapid decrease, the rate along a prescribed direction,
and the directions of no change.
</p>

<exercise xml:id="ex-electrostatic-plate">
  <title>Electric Potential on a Coated Plate</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      The electric potential on a thin coated plate, in volts, is
      <md>V(x,y) = 100 - 2x^2 - 6y^2,</md>
      with <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> in centimeters. Consider the point <m>P_0(2,1)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find <m>\nabla V</m> at <m>P_0</m> and the electric field
            <m>\mathbf E = -\nabla V</m> there. Along which unit vector does the
            potential drop most rapidly, and at what rate?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A test charge is nudged along
            <m>\hat{u} = \tfrac{4}{5}\hat{\imath} - \tfrac{3}{5}\hat{\jmath}</m>. Compute
            <m>D_{\hat{u}}V</m> at <m>P_0</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find the two unit vectors along which the potential does not change, and name
            the curve they are tangent to.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  
  </statement>
  <answer>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\nabla V = -4x\,\hat{\imath} - 12y\,\hat{\jmath}</m>, so
            <m>(\nabla V)_{(2,1)} = -8\,\hat{\imath} - 12\,\hat{\jmath}</m> and
            <m>\mathbf E = 8\,\hat{\imath} + 12\,\hat{\jmath}</m> V/cm. The potential drops
            fastest along <m>\hat{u} = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{13}}(2\,\hat{\imath} + 3\,\hat{\jmath})</m>,
            at the rate <m>-\|\nabla V\| = -4\sqrt{13} \approx -14.4</m> V/cm.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <md>D_{\hat{u}}V = (-8)\left(\tfrac{4}{5}\right)
                + (-12)\left(-\tfrac{3}{5}\right) = \tfrac{4}{5} = 0.8</md>
            in V/cm. The potential rises slightly along this heading.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\hat{u} = \pm\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{13}}(3\,\hat{\imath} - 2\,\hat{\jmath})</m>. These are
            tangent to the equipotential ellipse <m>2x^2 + 6y^2 = 14</m> through <m>P_0</m>,
            and they are orthogonal to <m>\mathbf E</m>, as they must be.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    <p component="print">
      An interactive figure of the plate <mdash/> the equipotential route at
      <m>V = 86</m> V and the field-line route side by side, with the
      potential read out as the point moves <mdash/> accompanies the online
      version of these notes.
    </p>
    <sidebyside component="web" width="100%">
    <figure xml:id="figure-potential-plate">
      <caption>The potential <m>V(x,y) = 100 - 2x^2 - 6y^2</m> on the plate. One route holds <m>V = 86</m> V along the equipotential <m>2x^2 + 6y^2 = 14</m>; the other follows a field line, on which <m>D_{\hat{u}}V = -\|\nabla V\|</m> at every point.</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-potential-plate"
                   iframe="potential-plate-3d.html"
                   width="100%" aspect="16:10"/>
    </figure>
    </sidebyside>
  </answer>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-isobars-low">
  <title>Sailing the Isobars of a Low</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Near the center of a low-pressure system the sea-level pressure, in millibars, is
      modeled by
      <md>P(x,y) = 988 + 0.004\left(x^2 + y^2\right),</md>
      with <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> in miles from the center of the low. A ship sits at
      <m>P_0(20,10)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find the pressure at the ship and <m>\nabla P</m> there. The
            pressure-gradient force per unit volume of air is a positive multiple of
            <m>-\nabla P</m>; in which unit direction does it push, and what is
            <m>\|\nabla P\|</m> in millibars per mile?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A second ship 40 miles from the center reports a stronger wind. Using this
            model, explain why <m>\|\nabla P\|</m> supports that report.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The captain decides to hold a constant barometer reading. Give the two
            possible unit headings at <m>P_0</m>, and describe the shape of the full route.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  
  </statement>
  <answer>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>P(20,10) = 988 + 0.004(500) = 990</m> mb, and
            <m>\nabla P = 0.008x\,\hat{\imath} + 0.008y\,\hat{\jmath}</m> gives
            <m>(\nabla P)_{(20,10)} = 0.16\,\hat{\imath} + 0.08\,\hat{\jmath}</m>. The force
            points along
            <m>-\mathbf u = -\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{5}}(2\,\hat{\imath} + \hat{\jmath})</m>, that is,
            inward toward the low, and
            <m>\|\nabla P\| = 0.08\sqrt{5} \approx 0.18</m> mb/mi.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Here <m>\|\nabla P\| = 0.008\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m> grows linearly with distance from
            the center, so at 40 miles it is <m>0.32</m> mb/mi, nearly double the value at
            the first ship. (Real storms reverse this trend close to the eye; the model is
            only reasonable over a limited annulus.)
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\mathbf u = \pm\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{5}}(\hat{\imath} - 2\,\hat{\jmath})</m>. Since the
            level curves of <m>P</m> are the circles <m>x^2+y^2 = \text{constant}</m>,
            holding the barometer steady means circling the storm at a fixed radius of
            <m>\sqrt{500} \approx 22.4</m> miles.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    <p component="web">
      Use the interactive figure to explore the problem further geometrically,
      switching between the two headings and watching how the barometer responds as
      the ship moves.
    </p>
    <p component="print">
      An interactive figure of the pressure field <mdash/> the two headings
      switchable, with the barometer responding as the ship moves <mdash/>
      accompanies the online version of these notes.
    </p>
    <sidebyside component="web" width="100%">
    <figure xml:id="figure-isobars-low">
      <caption>The pressure field <m>P(x,y) = 988 + 0.004(x^2+y^2)</m> around a low. One heading circles the eye at constant <m>990</m> mb; the other runs outward along <m>\nabla P</m>. The distance readout shows why <m>\|\nabla P\| = 0.008r</m> grows with radius.</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-isobars-low"
                   iframe="isobars-low-3d.html"
                   width="100%" aspect="16:10"/>
    </figure>
    </sidebyside>
  </answer>
</exercise>

</worksheet>